  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nCurrently, troughing is centered to our east with a jet stream to \nour south. Surface high pressure is also centered just to our east. \nTropical Depression 9 is also just east of Mexico. This will \ncontinue to promote dry and seasonally cool temperatures into the \nmorning. Temperatures will warm well into the 70s to low 80s through \nthe day as eastward progression of high pressure increases southerly \nflow. Based on the latest National Hurricane Center (NHC) forecast, \nT.D. 9 is expected to strengthen into Tropical Storm Isaias and \ntrack northeast into the central Gulf through the day. By Thursday, \nheight rises are expected as troughing lifts to the northeast, \npromoting even warmer temperatures well into the 80s. It is during \nthis time, hurricane strength is forecast by the NHC and model \nguidance, along with a more northerly shift in the track. Heading \ninto Friday, hurricane strength is still anticipated with a landfall \nnear or east of New Orleans by Friday night. With the upper jet \nremaining just to the north, increasing shear has been noted, adding \nto weakening but an increase in track speed. Locally, warm and dry \nconditions will remain with a shift to northeasterly winds for \nFriday. \n\nThe weekend will be when our region sees impacts from the remnants \nof the tropical system as a track to our west is forecast on \nSaturday. At this time range, there is still considerable \nuncertainty in the track and degree of weakening due to shear. \nHowever, the most likely impact in our area will be the first \npotential mountain wave event of the season. With high pressure to\nour northeast, a strong MSLP gradient is likely, along with \nstrong southeasterly 850mb winds. While rain is expected with this\nsystem, a track of this nature would certainly limit totals while\npotentially producing warning-level winds in the mountains and \nfoothills Saturday night. It should be noted that some guidance \nforecast a more easterly and/or weaker track, which would limit \nmountain wave potential. Regardless, rainfall chances and overall \nimpacts will decrease by Sunday into early next week as the \nremnants dissipate and move away from the region. Based on current\ntrends, similar messaging will be maintained in the HWO.",
